cartridge "WIZARD TAG II/BOB BLACKMER/1987"

' WIZARD TAG II
' A ColecoVision game based on "Wizard Tag II" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy! July
' 1987 (Commodore 64, machine code at $C000; the sequel to his Wizard Tag,
' games/wtag). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (from the article
' and its castle map, checked against the machine code, orig/wtag2-ml.dis),
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision with Wizard Tag's tiles, layered sprites and
' sound effects.
'
' The game: a castle of twelve floors joined by ladders. Gather the eight
' keys, then climb to the top floor and tag the wizard, all against the
' clock: 2:30 to start. Snakes travel the length of floors 2, 6 and 10:
' one trips you and drags you along with it (time lost). Thrashing vines
' sweep part of floors 4 and 8: one flings you down to the ground floor.
' The keys of those two floors lie at the far ends, past the vine's sweep:
' work out when to slip by. Tag the wizard and he plays his guitar while
' time is added (less each level); then the snakes and vines are faster.
' Tag him on level 9 and you are the winner. The score is the levels
' completed and the time left.
'
' From the original (orig/): twelve floors; ladders between every pair,
' three (left, middle, right: C64 columns 17, 54, 91) and two (34, 74) in
' turn, three between the ground floor and floor 2 and between floors 11
' and 12; walking only on a floor, climbing only at a ladder; the hero
' starts on the ground floor near the right end; six keys at random places
' on floors 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 and two fixed ones: floor 4 at the right end
' (its vine sweeps right over C64 columns 77-106), floor 8 at the left end
' (its vine sweeps left over columns 32-4); the vine is safe up to the key
' and the ladder outside its sweep, but the ladder inside it is the only
' way to the key without crossing the vine; three snakes on floors 2, 6, 10
' moving together left to right and starting again at the left; the
' wizard on the top floor at a random place, tagged only with all eight
' keys; on the ladders nothing can touch you; a snake drags the hero along
' until it reaches the right wall; a vine carries him to the end of its
' sweep, then he falls to the ground floor; 2:30 on the clock, real
' seconds, running while he is dragged or falls; after a tag 100 seconds
' are added, 10 less each level; the snakes and vines step every 8 passes
' at level 1, one pass sooner each level down to every pass; the keys are
' scattered again and the hero starts again on the ground floor; on level
' 9 the tag wins ("WE HAVE A WINNER !"); out of time ends the game.
'
' Liberties: the C64 scrolled a 22 x 13 window round the hero; here the
' castle is drawn the full width of the screen, six floors at a time, and
' the view moves three floors when he climbs near its edge (rows 3-20); a
' castle gauge (row 21) shows all twelve floors: keys still there, snakes,
' vines, the wizard and the hero, as the article's map does (the original
' had none: the map was in the magazine); the castle's 107 C64 columns are
' 208 pixels, smooth: the hero walks 0.75 pixel and climbs 1.5 pixels a
' frame (the C64's loop ran about 22 times a second, a column or a row a
' pass); the snakes and vines move by fractions of a pixel at the same pace
' as on the C64 (0.09 pixel a frame at level 1), but the vines never faster
' than their level 5 pace (0.19 a frame), so that slipping past them stays
' possible for a human (on the C64 at full speed it took a pixel-perfect
' swap); he steps onto a ladder
' within 6 pixels of it; the snake drags him while it crosses the floor, so
' a snake at level 1 costs up to 40 seconds as on the C64; touching the
' wizard without the eight keys says so; a side button pauses; the end
' shows the levels completed and the time left, the best kept on the
' title; title tune, guitar riff and sounds are new; the mail-in contest
' of the article is left out.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Left and right walk along a floor, up and down
' climb a ladder (stand at it), a side button pauses and starts the next
' level. Either controller. On the title, up and down choose, a button
' starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait. Music first in the sound table, effects after; the title
' tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear sprites`. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

